`lib/WWW/MailboxOrg/JSONRPCRequest.pm`: `to_hash` always writes the id member:

    my %hash = (
        jsonrpc => $self->jsonrpc,
        method  => $self->method,
        id      => $self->id,
    );

`WWW::MailboxOrg::Role::HTTP::notification()` builds the request with `id => undef`, and `lib/WWW/MailboxOrg/LWPIO.pm:62` sends `encode_json($req->to_hash)`. A notification therefore goes out as `{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"mail.send","params":{...},"id":null}`.

JSON-RPC 2.0 defines a notification as a Request object *without* an "id" member. "id": null is not a notification - it is a request with a null id, and a server may answer it with an error or wait for an answer that never comes. `notification()` is documented as "fire-and-forget (no response ID expected)".

Done when: a notification is serialised without any id member (the key is absent, not null), the request builder and the transport agree on that, and a test asserts the encoded body of a notification has no "id" key while a call still has one. The test has to be red against the current lib/.

